<p>EDIT: Redid this reply to make my position more explicit. TW: sexual assault, nonconsensual sex.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>My problem is that the article ends up lifting entire sections and concepts from SCP-166, but doesn't nail the graft. For instance, the Douay-Rheims Bible, is one that's really only used by the Catholic Church, and would, in my opinion, be about as vivid a reminder of the prior abuse as a clerical collar or crucifix. A better idea would be to use one of the more modern translations, such as the New International Version or Today's English Version (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modern_English_Bible_translations">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modern_English_Bible_translations</a>). Either that, or if SCP-4166 is more inclined to a scholarly approach, they might invest in the encyclopedia-sized Interpreter's Bible.</p>
<p>(Actually, let's be honest: in modern times, they could probably ask for an E-Reader and a whole crapload of different translations of the Bible instead, which is probably what someone who is seeking out a personal relationship with Christ outside of any prescribed canon or catechism might do.)</p>
<p>As for the title: the word &quot;succubus&quot; tends to be loaded, kind of like &quot;nymphet,&quot; in that it often ascribes agency and seduction to the woman in question. Considering that agency doesn't exist here when it comes to the woman in question being a victim, its use in this case feels a bit &quot;off,&quot; especially considering the traditional iconography of the succubus as a literal rapist of men.</p>
<p>Speaking of which, the traditional succubus iconography doesn't really include using men's semen for personal energy: that's a modern affectation. Traditional succubi were, as stated above, literal rapists of men who would sit on their faces and force them to perform cunnilingus on them, harvest their semen, and either transfer them to incubi to forcibly impregnate sleeping women with, or turn into incubi themselves. The demon's traditional iconography and feel have more to do with night terrors and wet dreams than the current &quot;sexy girl with wings&quot; approach.</p>
<p>Hm. It does occur to me that an argument can be made for SCP-4166-2 being the succubus, and not SCP-4166? After all, in that case we would be looking at a demon that traditionally attacks people in their sleep acting in a protective manner towards a person who made a literal deal with the devil with them to escape a horrifying situation&#8230;</p>
<p>Next, the origin. That's a literal holdover from Clef's backstory with the Cornwall Goddess: the convent in that case was a GOC-associated organization that took in 166 after Clef needed to find a safe place to drop her off. It fits in badly with the new take on 4166 here.</p>
<p>Hope I've made my suggestions and arguments clear.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

