			<div class="content" id="post-content-3921430">
									

<p>Overall enjoy this reimagining (or would rewrite be a better term?) of <a href="/scp-166">SCP-166</a>. Couple details seem a little off key for me though.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Open&nbsp;my&nbsp;long&nbsp;winded&nbsp;critique</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close&nbsp;my&nbsp;ramblings</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>◾A copy of the Holy Bible (Douay-Rheims, Challoner Revision) (Granted 08-01-2008, Removed by request 08-02-2008, Granted again 08-04-2008.).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not sure why she would want a bible, especially after reading that therapy log. If she hates the church as much as she says she does, why would she want a bible (point is general, but applies especially to the Douay-Rheims)?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4166-1 requires 1 cc of human semen weekly, administered via capsule with food.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not entirely sure why this detail was kept in, considering the succubus is more humanized this time around, being more of a human with anomalous properties about her rather than being the nymph-like creature that DrClef originally wrote her as. She's not borderline fapbait anymore, she's a victim of sexual abuse. Why does she need cum to survive? It is never explained, or even alluded to, so the inclusion of this detail seems tangential at best and unnecessary at worst. The principle of a certain <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chekhov%27s_gun">wall-mounted rifle</a> comes to mind&#8230;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4166-1 was raised by the nuns for twelve years prior to the hospitalization of Father █████ in an apparent case of spontaneous combustion. Six days afterwards, the first manifestation of SCP-4166-2 occurred on 07-24-2008.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Very minor issue here, but perhaps you should specify if the priest had died before or after the first manifestation of SCP-4166-2, because the way it is worded here is ambiguous about that information.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Upon entering the room, they found SCP-4166-1 alone, holding a pistol at the entrance to its room. Authorities were not notified of this incident.</p>
<p>A Foundation operative consulting with a local priest regarding an unrelated matter heard of the incidents and proceeded to the scene. When he entered the room of SCP-4166-1, he inadvertently triggered a manifestation of SCP-4166-2 before dispatching it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>All of this implies that -2 entities are as vulnerable to physical damage as a regular person would be, but the whole document is pretty tight-lipped about what these things are beyond &quot;shadowy humanoids&quot;. It doesn't mention where they go when they &quot;die&quot;, how they react to being physically attacked, or what happens if -1 cannot defend herself from them. This information is either left out entirely or is only mildly implied far later in the document than it should be. Maybe consider adding a little bit more to the description pertaining to these entities?</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close&nbsp;my&nbsp;ramblings</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Minor criticism aside, however, I think I like this article a tad bit more than the original. I like that the &quot;succubus&quot; is treated as a serious and vulnerable human character with a very fucked up, yet grounded backstory, as opposed to the mythological sex fairy that she originally was. It's currently a +1 from me, but I still would like to at least hear your thoughts on my criticisms, even if you just chalk them up to stylistic choices.</p>
<p>EDIT: After thinking about it for a little, changing to no vote for the time being.<br />
EDIT MKII: Back to a +1</p>

							</div>

								
